  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe above-normal temperatures and dry airmass will continue \ninto the beginning portion of the week, with afternoon highs\nreaching the upper 80s to low 90s through Tuesday. Did end up\nlowering highs from the NBM deterministic by a couple degrees on\nTuesday to account for some frontal boundary positioning\nuncertainty. Regardless, we'll be challenging record high\ntemperatures through Tuesday (see Climate section below). \n\nThe increased winds will continue on Monday, with southwesterly\ngusts up to 35mph west of Highway 65 and up to 20-30mph east of\nthe highway. This will once again contribute to elevated fire\nweather concerns, with the greatest threat east of Highway 65\nwhere minimum RHs drop into the 25-35% range. \n\nSynoptically, an upper level closed low and associated trough \nwill continue dropping into the Pacific Northwest region today \nbefore pushing southeast towards the Rockies. It's currently \nprogged to ingest Tropical Storm Rachel once she makes landfall \noff the coast of CA/northern Baja Peninsula and pushes over the\ndesert southwest late Tuesday. Latest guidance seems to have \nslowed this system down a bit, with large spreads between the \nmodels regarding the systems evolution/timing leading to \ncontinued uncertainty. Current thinking has a cold front \ndropping south into the area late Tuesday before stalling out \nand lifting in the form of a warm front Wednesday night, with a \nsecondary cold front sweeping through Friday morning, ending the\nprecipitation chances. Of course, there are still timing \nuncertainties. Regardless, this will bring our next chance of \nshowers and storms as early as Tuesday evening/night (30-60%), \nwith the highest chances (65-85%) on Wednesday before the front \nlifts northeast Thursday (30-60% pops) and precipitation ends on\nFriday with the secondary front. As the previous discussion \nmentioned, this is a fairly complex forecast with many \ndiscrepancies. Therefore, it's still too early to get into the \nweeds of specific rainfall amounts, with details continuing to \nget ironed out in future updates.\n\nBehind the cold frontal passage, cooler temperatures will move\nin by next weekend.",
